question/math-academy-id "q-333272" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07399bf6-90a9-5db9-8898-b1137d5bdf22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425272} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6b2dc2-3274-5ad6-936b-bec7b72f7ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c52