& ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y &=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815f28d1-80c5-5970-a177-82ef4375787f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164372} {:answer/id #uuid "893bfcd0-5816-5d8b-82e8-178257a58afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^