/id #edb/ref 17592186533547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533389} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4487a4-144f-5b03-86d5-0ad215d73311" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533966}] :question/id #uuid "d04cdee5-5005-58d2-afad-7aaebd536510" :question/math-academy-id "q-333601" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2b6588-f32d-5385-bd36-7b8f041a190c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550480} {:answer/id #uuid "683fbbc1-c806-50d9-85a2-308dfd7a7a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550627} {:answer/id #uuid "75aa5e5b-700c-53ad-aec6-1adbaed46b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550663} {:answer/id #uuid "a76d076d-96cf-5f10-aa9f-cad8b99ef4fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550663} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b526db-cf02-5f36-a044-7d6b75922f0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "591b2b7e-b01c-5e07-a746-ed180d35a0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592