ligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166547 :question/id #uuid "4aa5583f-003b-55a7-bfac-ae42ac44a380" :question/math-academy-id "e-21645" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n![](images/dd/dda3aceadd2c3bc3b53422d9533f3b5926906de522137d2e0edc4794d658b1fd.svg)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90771919-fe3a-5feb-8f43-29a2eba3450c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164382} {:answer/id #uuid "95cc0d88-f4f6-52b8-bff7-815d9494543e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164383} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d47f9d-027f-5655-9b04-aa092645db06" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "965806e1-be1a-5edc-97dc-84aff040e3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164385} {:answer/id #uuid "fb61e42f-7627-523b-8977-123ee7e44576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164386} :answer-field/id #uuid "857925f5-19ce-57b7-9662-2aa8862cf264" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164384}] :question/id #uuid "00e880d4-f09c-5b80-8d65-2954cf7b3f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-332768" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fe96278d09a5875c2f1e7c8762a808fd9549b37508f70b129b20e311b166308.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223862d4-0ce2-5e4a-ba6c-0e2e05a1a8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305478} {:answer/id #uuid "f474d111-156d-5bf5-b3cc-c97463a138bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307413} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7355ff-9284-5fde-81e0-bca9bc975af3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8996b55-40b3-5d46-a519-3a9f273ef6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307298} {:answer/id #uuid "ef13a079-43ab-5ec4-bf18-9df035d64273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307345} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8bb0cc5-f12a-525d-a29f-a4a6e34ca991" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308312}] :question/id #uuid "b699002a-13ee-52d0-9a5f-852554c5f030" :question/math-academy-id "q-333672" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =1$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0e/0e2180fe05500bcff2d0a1e2c066e2364eea0fd481a63866578a07e8f46b3825.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0badaf72-c8bb-5b1e-a5e0-b876dcb7d737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305251} {:answer/id #uuid "86ba88b4-1dd8-540d-a0f5-393a08ba558b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306436} {:answer/id #uuid "ed83be68-4ebc-5b94-8825-70fad6c49fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306436} :answer-field/id #uuid "35040c4d-4af1-5e38-8159-d511e076aaae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c2bd2e-e654-5241-a63e-de97ad1316e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306096} {:answer/id #uuid "76432db1-a96f-5377-906e-44f1c2094796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306276} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8e0205-9288-54d6-9f64-5d7634051eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306276} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc7fc02-3df4-5ce2-9b4d-6af4a9b818db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307964}] :question/id #uuid "701d41a8-54c4-5085-a286-95e1ba1a0460" :question/math-academy-id "q-333619" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/21/21b115b90b81f2f1bf3ecad3c8e200dae0ccd50c84459e047297e70e22efcb6f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb5eb6-00b1-5824-aac1-9875d7fe66e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305874} {:answer/id #uuid "caf7d461-ff75-53ec-a868-28af19c3a5d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307035} {:answer/id #uuid "e46fcc1d-38f3-5984-ae46-bff18bf82d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307035} :answer-field/id #uuid "09766c90-fc59-51b7-915d-02bb4b00d085" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1aaa80-46dc-5f8f-8c5c-a5903761c3ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305248} {:answer/id #uuid "32913ff0-0b12-5e4f-92cb-1a554cc18eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305628} {:answer/id #uuid "34f76203-01ef-5b8f-b51d-78acc82a48e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305248} :answer-field/id #uuid "304deb61-a235-5743-a7d4-56f1d6f329bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307673}] :question/id #uuid "f8861ee7-e573-5d09-b7e5-a9dfbae7172d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333620" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97a85038b240896686ef1a796d81acc6aa52d01627f3aeca4268a309badc4867.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1750bded-03a2-535d-9322-af370f69f5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391221} {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222} :answer-field/id #uuid "23101e77-153a-53ab-8eaa-fb91f151bfef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} {:answer/id #uuid "79852ea3-d668-55cf-ae57-0e55042a72c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5874e815-f2f7-5127-b199-5dfa1e8dd28e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391223}] :question/id #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" :question/math-academy-id "q-333614" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a91b22-3c2e-591c-95dd-7d31a5689a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391259} {:answer/id #uuid "c3746fa1-fc10-5bb5-88e6-d4d1facc4dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391259} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e7eaec-059c-5d14-9f8c-61c1f07a19ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391258} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0901b7b-91df-5d1e-af60-9e839083f14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391262} {:answer/id #uuid "37c33283-6fbe-5f50-a58a-dfc0bc8c628a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391262} :answer-field/id #uuid "32217f1b-d451-5dd0-8e1b-fafec5464418" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391261}] :question/id #uuid "13d1d15c-2bcc-581d-b50d-d0762931b97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333678" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-1$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92efcce675a436ad349ca6139cb12cda88fbdd5bdeaf2a5f1e755e7ba1f6d037.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bffdd4a-dba1-5509-8e3d-c18282eca373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425300} {:answer/id #uuid "d5288d39-b196-5969-af50-0a4f38dc67d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425300} :answer-field/id #uuid "3895b1d1-8fde-5e9c-8254-eb743b48b9d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f3f427d-a11c-5581-802d-503fcc0ff60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425290} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cc45aa-4540-5bbd-9f20-b56a06897950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425315} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ba5050-79f5-5038-b180-5f46df5f54fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425323}] :question/id #uuid "6510dfcd-a99e-5730-8a08-a25b49094096" :question/math-academy-id "q-333674" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =3$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/95/95bb5be106524711e86d2e1ff9cb21d61141c25eb0a2f8958b51c29791806ccc.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15abd56-8ff1-5940-9908-64440b2f7303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425302} {:answer/id #uuid "f89e8a56-5742-5f30-a939-70f76c6deb0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425302} :answer-field/id #uuid "3007b7e8-b0aa-517c-b7ed-d47324656b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dead613-6310-5d03-ae0a-f9d87e3caec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425273} {:answer/id #uuid "24e84d6a-bff5-5bb2-b795-aa9cf9834a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425278} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63bded0-5eca-5ef0-8469-313f1af281d1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425333}] :question/id #uuid "fc362b82-7598-58e4-b9cc-cf1f2a033f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-333667" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-2$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/94/94f722ae71911d6b6967e55922301e771753078302aef950e1215d1fead75289.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65ac0981-1b1d-5c23-9cc0-fc444715eee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9a387f-3c17-5e04-b07f-ec5133f1dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c5a737-0591-5fd5-8df7-bba8f7661150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70dfdf1e-bde5-58a0-8f24-d392080fc911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dff475-5ce9-583a-91b1-fe2ebf698ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} :answer-field/id #uuid "563869eb-e9b6-592a-b3a5-fad42529fb9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533848}] :question/id #uuid "130e77e3-e68d-5bfc-a672-f433d0d78798" :question/math-academy-id "q-333626" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3240 :topic/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060636} :activity/id #uuid "ab353e02-ddaa-5627-8691-ddb0bfb2272a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060926 :tutorial/content "We can multiply matrices by numbers. This type of multiplication is known as **scalar multiplication**. Here, a **scalar** is just a number.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo multiply ${A}$ by the scalar $2,$ we just multiply each entry of ${A}$ by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to multiply a $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ by a scalar $c,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $c$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & … & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & … & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & … & {a}_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d7efb59b-c6d7-5c06-97f3-b19ec863c9eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2975 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef152b9a-eaba-5d9c-a8a6-e416423ed6d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4cc58a6-ebb8-544d-93a1-de6cbd5b180e"} :step/id #uuid "6daf12ec-1eb5-57ef-a3ef-c7a9d1b2b280" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2da3580a-6a1a-5e38-bcab-bd35f1ea5a6e"} :step/id #uuid "7a5087ae-4093-5cff-86c6-08085031421e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6727fdd-a614-5ead-9273-08c0a7fec997"} :step/id #uuid "bf6aee0d-bc4e-5730-8677-5e95a4ce6077"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060927 :tutorial/content "Since scalar multiplication is done entry-wise, it obeys the same kind of rules as multiplication of real numbers.\n\nFor example, for any numbers $c_1, c_2$ and any matrix $A,$ we can perform scalar multiplication in any order: \n\n$$\nc_1 (c_2 A) = (c_1 c_2) A = c_2 (c_1 A)\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can distribute a matrix over the sum or difference of scalars:\n\n$$\n(c_1 \\pm c_2)A = c_1A \\pm c_2A\n$$\n\nWe can also distribute scalar multiplication over the sum or difference of two matrices:\n\n$$\nc (A \\pm B) = c A \\pm c B\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "93b75b66-36ff-5b1f-b39f-9c76b6bed09c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2977 :tutorial/title "Properties of Scalar Multiplication of Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "72307be6-02e4-5c60-95a7-5eea2a93b8ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060639}}] :activity/title "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059880}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059549 :question/id #uuid "4391bd7a-560e-5b59-bf5c-b68ff235c05e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4380" :question/problem "Calculate $-2A$, if \n$A=\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\begin{matrix}\n        \t2 & -1  \\\\\n        \t4 & 7\n    \t\\end{matrix}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\cdot 2 & -2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -2\\cdot 4 & -2\\cdot 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4cc58a6-ebb8-544d-93a1-de6cbd5b180e" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539284 :question-generator/id #uuid "da946ccc-fca7-555f-b463-0d2178a05780" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186059740.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8868f167-119b-5e73-bc65-40becaf6c2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059335} {:answer/id #uuid "e74bb0c6-bc63-5059-84dc-eb9b59596b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059336} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f4f4af-05d0-53f1-8ebb-4276ab2ed9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059337} {:answer/id #uuid "21f238e5-ba0c-57b7-8096-4d2e15910cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059338} {:answer/id #uuid "51967300-99d9-5ef0-a07f-ecc5ed2d01a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059339}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a645da-29fd-5221-a5e3-74f151b09fe2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059334}] :qu