/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336523} {:answer/id #uuid "481b0866-7058-503a-a734-590463395c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336699} {:answer/id #uuid "704bb3ac-a676-5591-af22-a855345dbbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,2,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337030} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7dfa65-88da-5a46-b2ca-a933aa7923a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337696} :answer-field/id #uuid "227aa067-36e4-59b1-ab9c-c3bdc32b6208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338342}] :question/id #uuid "2bb7afc4-6665-54d6-aaac-0d0fbaf7ddf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-71250" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+z-3 &=0 \\\\ 3x+2y-5 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e5cebb-5d0e-5d45-823b-9b1b16df98cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336200} {:answer/id #uuid "18c29390-c1d8-57b2-b751-73d7fa2f74f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer